Build a Multi-User Markdown Notes App with Next.js 16 and MongoDB

Act as a senior full-stack engineer. Build a complete, production-minded Notes application using Next.js 16, MongoDB, and TypeScript. The app should allow multiple users to register, sign in, and securely manage their own Markdown notes.

Prioritize clean architecture, a polished user interface, secure authentication, and a fully functional end-to-end implementation. Do not build just a mockup or static prototype.

1. Technology Stack

Use the following technologies:

Framework: Next.js 16 with the App Router.
Language: TypeScript.
Project configuration: Use the default Next.js settings and conventions. Keep the standard project structure and configuration generated by create-next-app. Use the default recommended settings for TypeScript, ESLint, Tailwind CSS, and the @/* import alias.
Styling: Tailwind CSS using the version and configuration compatible with the generated Next.js project.
Database: MongoDB.
Database integration: Mongoose for schemas, models, validation, and database operations.
Authentication: Auth.js or a compatible, secure authentication solution supporting email/password registration and login.
Markdown editor: A well-maintained React Markdown editor with live preview and Markdown formatting support.
Icons: Lucide React.
Validation: Zod for validating user input on both client and server where appropriate.

Use compatible, stable package versions. Do not downgrade Next.js or replace the requested stack with another framework.

Keep the implementation aligned with Next.js 16 conventions, including current App Router, Server Components, Server Actions, Route Handlers, and caching APIs where appropriate. Do not blindly copy patterns from older Next.js versions.

2. Application Overview

Build a personal notes application where multiple independent users can create accounts and manage their own notes.

Each user must only be able to access their own notes. Users must never be able to view, modify, or delete another user's notes by manipulating URLs, IDs, requests, or API calls.

The application should feel like a modern productivity tool: minimal, fast, responsive, and easy to navigate.

3. Authentication and User Management

Implement a complete authentication flow.

Registration
Allow users to register with their name, email address, and password.
Validate all fields on the server.
Ensure email addresses are unique.
Hash passwords securely using Argon2id or bcrypt.
Never store plaintext passwords.
Provide useful validation and error messages.
Login and logout
Allow registered users to sign in using their email and password.
Use secure session management.
Use HTTP-only, secure cookies in production, with appropriate SameSite settings.
Implement logout functionality.
Protect all authenticated pages and server-side operations.
Redirect unauthenticated users to the login page when they attempt to access protected features.
User settings
Display the current user's name and email.
Provide a profile menu with logout functionality.
Keep each user's notes isolated through server-side ownership checks.

Do not implement social login unless needed for a working authentication solution. Email/password authentication is the primary requirement.

4. Notes Management

Implement full CRUD functionality: Create, Read, Update, and Delete.

Each note should contain:

title: string, required.
content: Markdown content stored as a string.
userId: reference to the owning user.
createdAt: creation timestamp.
updatedAt: last modification timestamp.
isPinned: boolean, default false.
isArchived: boolean, default false.
tags: array of strings, default empty.
Create notes
Provide a button to create a new note.
Allow users to enter a title and Markdown content.
Save notes to MongoDB.
Assign ownership using the authenticated user's ID on the server.
Provide a clear save state and success or error feedback.
Read notes
Display the authenticated user's notes in a clean list or grid.
Show the note title, a short content preview, tags, and last-updated date.
Sort notes by most recently updated by default.
Display pinned notes separately or before regular notes.
Provide a dedicated view for reading individual notes.
Update notes
Allow users to edit existing notes.
Preserve Markdown formatting when loading and saving content.
Update the updatedAt timestamp.
Support a clear save action and, if practical, autosave with debouncing and visible saving/saved/error states.
Prevent accidental loss of unsaved changes.
Delete notes
Provide a delete action with a confirmation dialog.
Permanently remove the selected note from MongoDB.
Verify ownership on the server before deleting.
Pin and archive
Allow users to pin and unpin notes.
Allow users to archive and restore notes.
Keep archived notes out of the default active-notes view.
Provide dedicated navigation for archived notes.

