8. MongoDB Data Models

Create well-structured Mongoose models.

User model

Include:

Name.
Normalized, unique email.
Password hash.
Created and updated timestamps.
Note model

Include:

Title.
Markdown content.
User reference.
Tags.
Pinned status.
Archived status.
Created and updated timestamps.

Add appropriate indexes, including indexes for user ownership, sorting, and commonly used filters. Ensure uniqueness constraints are enforced at the database level where applicable.

Create a reusable MongoDB connection utility that caches the connection during development to prevent unnecessary connections during hot reloads.

Use an environment variable such as:

MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/markdown_notes

Keep all database access on the server. Never expose database credentials or privileged database operations to client-side code.

9. Application Architecture

Organize the application into clear, maintainable modules.

Use:

Next.js App Router for routing.
Server Components for data-driven pages wherever practical.
Client Components only for interactive features such as the editor, dialogs, and responsive navigation.
Server Actions or Route Handlers for mutations and API operations, choosing the approach that best fits each feature.
Reusable UI components.
Shared validation schemas.
A centralized database connection.
Reusable authentication and authorization utilities.

A suggested structure is:

app/ — pages, layouts, loading states, error states, and route handlers.
components/ — sidebar, note cards, editor, Markdown preview, dialogs, and shared UI.
lib/ — database connection, authentication, validation, and utilities.
models/ — Mongoose User and Note models.
actions/ — server actions for note and account operations, if appropriate.
types/ — shared TypeScript types.

Adapt the structure to idiomatic Next.js 16 conventions rather than creating unnecessary abstractions.

10. Security Requirements

Security is mandatory.

Enforce authentication on all private note operations.
Derive the owner ID from the authenticated server-side session, never from a trusted client-supplied userId.
Verify note ownership on every read, update, archive, pin, and delete operation.
Use server-side validation for all untrusted inputs.
Never expose password hashes, session secrets, or MongoDB credentials.
Protect against NoSQL injection by validating input and constructing queries safely.
Prevent mass-assignment vulnerabilities by explicitly selecting mutable fields.
Sanitize Markdown rendering.
Apply reasonable limits to title length, content size, and tags.
Use secure session and cookie settings.
Return appropriate error responses without leaking sensitive server details.
Ensure private note data is not accidentally cached or shared between users.

Do not rely solely on middleware or client-side redirects for authorization. Every sensitive data operation must independently verify the authenticated user's identity and permissions.

11. Performance and Reliability

Avoid unnecessary client-side JavaScript.
Use pagination or incremental loading when note collections become large.
Add appropriate MongoDB indexes.
Prevent duplicate submissions.
Handle database connection failures gracefully.
Provide clear loading and error states.
Use optimistic UI updates only when failures can be handled safely.
Ensure the app builds successfully with TypeScript and the project's configured linting tools.

Do not introduce caching for private user-specific data unless it is explicitly scoped and safe.

